DO  -  10.1002/rth2.12229
AB  -  Background : There are scant data on the outcomes of cerebral venous thrombosis (CVT) in cancer population. Aims : We examined the recanalization, thrombosis recurrence and major bleeding during CVT treatment in a cancer exclusive adult population. Methods : A retrospective cohort study of cancer patients with CVT identified through an institutional data warehouse at MD Anderson Cancer Center between January 2002 and June 2017. Patients with other indications for long term anticoagulation were excluded. The primary end point was thrombus recanalization at 12 months. Secondary end points were major bleeding and recurrent venous thrombosis at 12 months. Demographic data, cancer diagnosis and therapy were obtained at the time of CVT. Variables were compared across subgroups of study outcomes. The backward stepdown procedure was used to identify variables for the final logistic model regarding thrombosis and bleeding outcomes. Computations were carried out in SAS version 9.4. Results : The population included predominantly male adults (55.6%) with a median age of 54.5 years. Solid malignancies comprised 64.4% of cases. Anticoagulation was prescribed in 73.3% of cases, with a median duration of treatment of 7.9 months. Partial and complete recanalization were achieved in 33.3% and 17.8% of cases, respectively. The thrombosis recurrence rate at 12 months was 15.6%. We identified no clinical or treatment‐ related variables associated with thrombosis recurrence. A total of 11 patients suffered major bleeding. Treatment with warfarin and overweight (BMI>27) were associated with bleeding. For warfarin, the odds of bleeding was substantially higher compared to LMWH [OR=7.55, CI 1.05‐ 54.33, p= .045]. Conclusions : Our findings suggest that LMWH is an effective and safe strategy for the management cancer‐ CVT. The findings are consistent with bleeding outcomes from large randomized trials of cancer associated venous thrombosis. Limitations to our study are the retrospective and non‐ randomized design for the adjudication of treatment agents.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01980937/full
