AB  -  Background: The FDA label for apixaban for prevention of venous thromboembolism (VTE) recurrence includes a dose reduction to 2.5 mg twice daily after 6 months of anticoagulation therapy. Whether this dose reduction is advisable for cancer associated VTE is unknown. Aims: We tested the hypothesis that apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily is associated with a lower combined rate of major bleed plus clinically relevant nonmajor bleeding (CRNMB) compared to apixaban 5 mg twice daily for the secondary prevention of VTE in patients with active cancer who have completed 6 to 12 months of anticoagulation. Methods: This phase III, multicenter, randomized, double‐blind, investigatorinitiated, trial assessed combined rates of major bleeding plus CRNMB (primary outcome). Treatment duration was 12 months. Secondary outcomes were VTE recurrence or arterial thromboembolism. Results: Of 370 patients recruited, the results from 360 were analyzed. The four most common tumor sites were gastrointestinal, lung, hematologic, and breast malignancies; 59.7% had metastasis. Major plus CRNMB occurred in 16 of 179 patients (8.9%) in the apixaban 2.5 mg group and in 22 of 179 patients (12.2%) in the 5 mg group (HR 0.72, 95% CI 0.38‐1.37, p = 0.31). Major bleeding occurred in 2.8% of the apixaban 2.5 mg group and 2.2% in the 5 mg group (HR 1.26, 95% CI 0.34‐4.66, p = 0.73). Recurrent VTE or arterial thrombosis occurred in 9 patients (5.0%) of each treatment group (HR 1.0 95% CI 0.40 – 2.53, p = 0.81). All‐cause mortality rates were similar between groups, 13.6% vs. 11.7% (HR 1.21, 95% CI 0.67 – 2.18; p = 0. 53). Conclusion(s): Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily among cancer patients with VTE resulted in similar rates of bleeding compared to 5 mg without increasing thrombotic outcomes. [Figure presented] [Figure presented]
