AB  -  Background : About 10‐ 15% of patients with superficial vein thrombosis (SVT) develop venous thromboembolic (VTE) complications at 3‐ 6 months, despite an adequate treatment of the acute phase. No data are available about pharmacological secondary prevention of SVT. Aims : To verify the superiority of a blend of glycosaminoglicans (mesoglycan) vs placebo in reducing the incidence of VTE complications in patients with SVT completing the recommended anticoagulant treatment course of the acute phase. Methods : Phase III multicentric, double‐ blind, randomized clinical trial comparing mesoglycan 50 mg bid vs placebo. Consecutive patients in 18 Italian hospital‐ based vascular centers aged > 18 y with objective diagnosis of SVT extended at least 5 cm and up to 3 cm from the sapheno‐ femoral junction, and completing an initial subcutaneous treatment course of 45 days with fondaparinux 2.5 mg od are included. Duration: 24 months (12 months of treatment plus 12 months of follow‐ up). Follow‐ up visits: 3,6,9,12,18,24 months with clinical evaluation; treatment compliance; VCSS and VEINES/Sym‐ QoL questionnaires assessment; ultrasound scan for any suspect of VTE; blood sample screening if needed. Primary efficacy outcome: SVT recurrence/extension, symptomatic venous thromboembolism and asymptomatic proximal deep‐ vein thrombosis, mortality. Primary safety outcome: major bleeding, serious adverse events. We hypotesized a 15% risk for the primary efficacy outcome in the placebo group at 12 months and a 50% risk reduction in the mesoglycan group. A bilateral log‐ rank test with a sample of 650 patients (randomization 1:1) reach a 90% power, with an α‐ error of 0.025, of detecting a 7.0% difference (HR=0.51) after 12 months of treatment, considering a 10% patients drop‐ out. Results : Currently 80 patients have been included and randomized with a 5% of dropped patients. Recruitment is still ongoing and is planned to finish in 18‐ 24 months. Conclusions : Recruitment of patients will be completed within Q2 2020.
