TI  -  2017 - In VTE, extending anticoagulation with rivaroxaban vs aspirin reduced recurrence without increasing bleeding
JA  -  ACP journal club
PY  -  2017
VL  -  166
IS  -  12
CC  -  Vascular
SP  -  4‐4
M3  -  Journal article
AB  -  Questions After 6 to 12 months of anticoagulation therapy for venous thromboembolism (VTE), what are the efficacy and safety of extended anticoagulation with rivaroxaban vs aspirin? Does efficacy or safety vary by dose of rivaroxaban? Methods Design Randomized controlled trial (Reduced‐dosed Rivaroxaban in the Long‐ term Prevention of Recurrent Symptomatic Venous Thromboembolism [EINSTEIN CHOICE] trial). ClinicalTrials.gov NCT02064439. Allocation Concealed.* Blinding Blinded* (patients, {health care providers, data collectors, data monitoring committee}†, and outcome adjudication committee). Follow‐up period 6 to 12 months (median 50 wk). Setting 244 centers in 31 countries. Patients 3396 patients =18 years of age (mean age 59 y, 55% men in 3365 patients in the intention‐to‐treat [ITT] population) who had a symptomatic, objectively confirmed proximal deep venous thrombosis or pulmonary embolism (PE) and had received anticoagulation therapy (vitamin K antagonist or direct oral anticoagulant) for 6 to 12 months without interruption for >7 days. Exclusion criteria included need for extended anticoagulation therapy at therapeutic doses or antiplatelet therapy, calculated creatinine clearance <30 mL/min, or hepatic disease with coagulopathy. Intervention Rivaroxaban, 20 mg/d (n=1121); rivaroxaban, 10 mg/d (n=1136); or aspirin, 100 mg/d (n=1139) for 6 to 12 months. Outcomes Primary efficacy outcome was a composite of symptomatic, recurrent fatal or nonfatal VTE or unexplained death potentially related to PE. Other outcomes included major bleeding and clinically relevant nonmajor bleeding. Patient follow‐up 99% were included in the ITT analysis. Main results Rivaroxaban, 20 mg/d or 10 mg/d, reduced VTE recurrence and did not increase major bleeding compared with aspirin; the 2 doses of rivaroxaban did not differ for either outcome (Table). Groups did not differ for clinically relevant nonmajor bleeding (rivaroxaban 20 mg, 2.7%; rivaroxaban 10 mg, 2.0%; aspirin 1.8%; P>0.05 for all comparisons). Conclusion In patients with venous thromboembolism, extending anticoagulation therapy with rivaroxaban vs aspirin reduced recurrence and did not increase major bleeding.
