AB  -  Objective Statement: Superficial venous thrombosis (SVT) is a common disorder often associated with Deep vein Thrombosis (DVT) or pulmonary embolism (PE) and which may, in the absence of appropriate treatment, be associated with a significant risk of DVT. In the context of reimbursement approvement of fondaparinux for the treatment of SVT, the French health Authority requested to carry out a study of outpatient management by vascular physicians. The objective was to describe the characteristics of patients (demographic, medical history and ultrasound features of the thrombus), treated for acute symptomatic lower limb spontaneous SVT in adults without concomitant DVTand to assess the incidence of recurrences of venous thrombo‐embolic events (VTE: DVT, PE, recurrent SVT or SVT extensions) and major hemorrhagic events on treatment, depending on initial management. Method: PERSEUS was a French observational cohort study based on 1,000 adult patients with acute symptomatic lower limb spontaneous SVT without concomitant DVT, included between July 2013 and February 2014, with a follow‐up of 3 months. The outcomes were VTE (DVT, PE, extension of SVT, recurrence of SVT in another location) and major bleeding events. Incidence of outcome was estimated using Kaplan Meier estimation. Results: 144 vascular physicians included 1,069 ambulatory patients, 978 of whom were followed up for 3 months. Average patient age was 63.3 years, 62.9% were women, 27.3% were obese, 92.8% had varicose veins, 34.2% had a past history of SVT, 17.8% had a past history of DVT or PE. 75.2% were treated with fondaparinux and 13.0% with low molecular weight heparin (LMWH), 2.9% with other systemic treatments and 9% did not receive a systemic treatment for SVT during the 48 hours following inclusion. 78.1% of the patients treated with fondaparinux received a dose of 2.5 mg and 4.4% received a high dose for less than 7 days before being given 2.5 mg. Median length of treatment on fondaparinux was 34 days. Incidence of VTE was 0.8% (95%CI, 0.3%‐1.8%) for patients treated with fondaparinux (6 patients with an event: 3 extensions of SVT, 2 recurrences of SVT and 1 DVT) and 2.4% (95%CI, 0.5%‐6.7%) or patients treated with LWMH (3 patients: 3 extensions of SVT, 2 with also recurrences in another localization and one with a DVT). No major hemorrhagic events were reported during the study for any treatment. Conclusions: The incidence of VTE with fondaparinux in this real life study was similar to that in the Calisto randomized trial (0.9%) despite an older population, higher prevalence of risk factors of VTE and past history of SVTand DVT or PE. The incidence point estimate of VTE is lower among patients treated with fondaparinux than in the LMWH group, but there is wide intersection of the 95% confidence intervals and the low number of cases observed did not make it possible to perform adequate comparisons. The high prevalence of past history shows the recurrent nature of SVT and that SVT should be considered a clinical component of venous thromboembolic disease.
