AB  -  Background: Up to half of the acute pulmonary embolism (PE) patients report persistent dyspnea and/or functional limitations despite adequate anticoagulant treatment, qualifying for post‐PE syndrome (PPES). Deconditioning due to physical inactivity after acute PE seems to be the most frequent etiology explaining PPES. Depressive disorders and fear for complications or recurrences, may further contribute to this downward spiral. Early exercise training after the acute PE may have positive effects on exercise capacity and quality of life in PE patients. Aims: To evaluate the efficacy and safety of exercise training initiated shortly after the acute PE diagnosis on physical performance and the prevention of development of PPES. Methods: The PE@HOME study is a randomized controlled, open label clinical trial. Patients with a recent PE diagnosis (< 4 weeks within start of treatment) who report incomplete functional recovery are eligible to participate. Included patients will perform a cardiopulmonary exercise test (CPET) and constant work rate cycle test (CWRT) at baseline. Patients with a baseline CWRT≤15 minutes will be randomized 1:1 to an 8‐week, homebased standardized exercise training program using a cycle ergometer, supervised by a board‐certified physiotherapist, or a control group in which patients receive general recommendations on healthy lifestyle and physical activity (Figure 1). After the intervention period, CPET and CWRT will be repeated to assess any improvement over time. Patients are followed for 6 months. The study has been approved by the Institutional Review Board Leiden‐Den Haag‐Delft. Results: The primary objective is to determine the effect of an 8‐week exercise training program on physical performance as assessed by CWRT. Secondary objectives include the incidence of PPES as assessed using patient‐reported outcome measures, the difference in physical activity and exercise physiology between start and end of the study, cost‐effectiveness of the 8‐week intervention, and safety of the intervention. Conclusion(s): N.A. [Figure presented]
