DO  -  10.1002/ajh.24471
AB  -  BACKGROUND The randomized phase III EINSTEIN DVT study tested rivaroxaban 15 mg twice daily for 21 days followed by 20 mg once daily thereafter for the treatment of deep vein thrombosis. This single‐drug approach is the approved treatment regimen for rivaroxaban. The non‐interventional XALIA study assessed the safety and effectiveness of rivaroxaban for treatment of deep vein thrombosis in routine clinical practice. In XALIA, treatment decisions were at the physician's discretion and several different rivaroxaban regimens emerged early in the treatment process. OBJECTIVES To describe the clinical characteristics and outcomes of a subset of patients from the XALIA study, designated as 'early switchers', who initially received heparin/ fondaparinux for >2‐14 days and/or a vitamin K antagonist (VKA) for 1‐14 days before switching to rivaroxaban. METHODS XALIA investigated the safety and effectiveness of rivaroxaban (n=2619) compared with standard anticoagulation therapy (initial treatment with unfractionated heparin, low molecular weight heparin, or fondaparinux, usually overlapping with and followed by a VKA) (n=2149) in patients with deep vein thrombosis (latterly with concomitant pulmonary embolism). The primary outcomes in XALIA were major bleeding, recurrent venous thromboembolism, and all‐cause mortality. The type, dose, and duration of therapy were at the physician's discretion. Patients who received rivaroxaban alone and those who had received heparin/ fondaparinux for a maximum of 48 hours before enrollment were included in the rivaroxaban cohort, consistent with the approach in EINSTEIN DVT. RESULTS In total, there were 368 'early switchers'; 325 switched from heparin/fondaparinux and 43 from a VKA. Compared with patients in the rivaroxaban cohort, the 'early switcher' group had more risk factors for recurrence or bleeding, including: creatinine clearance <50 mL/min (6.5% vs 3.9%), previous major bleeding events (4.6% vs 1.4%), and active cancer at baseline (8.2% vs 5.6%). They were also more likely to have concomitant pulmonary embolism (20.9% vs 8.4%). Compared with patients in the rivaroxaban cohort, the crude event rates were numerically higher in the 'early switchers' for major bleeding (1.4% vs 0.7%), recurrent venous thromboembolism (2.2% vs 1.4%), and all‐cause mortality (0.8% vs 0.5%). The median treatment duration for the 'early switchers' was 190 days (interquartile range [IQR] 107‐376 days) compared with 181 days (IQR 94‐310 days) in the rivaroxaban group, whereas the median length of stay in hospitalized patients was 7 days (IQR 5‐10 days) for the 'early switchers' versus 5 days (IQR 3‐7 days) for the patients in the rivaroxaban group. A sensitivity analysis including the 'early switchers' in the rivaroxaban cohort when comparing with the standard anticoagulation group did not affect the study outcomes. CONCLUSIONS The data from XALIA and this patient subgroup provide evidence that, in routine clinical practice, physicians exercised caution in their choice of therapy and often initially prescribed standard anticoagulation to fragile/high‐risk patients before starting rivaroxaban. The outcomes data may reflect the higher risk profile of this 'early switcher' population. This analysis suggests that patients can readily switch to rivaroxaban if parenteral agents are used during the initial period without compromising treatment safety and effectiveness.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01763750/full
