DO  -  10.1016/j.thromres.2018.02.036
AB  -  Introduction: Cancer patients with acute venous thromboembolism (VTE) are at increased risk of recurrent thrombosis and anticoagulant‐ associated bleeding compared to non‐cancer patients with VTE. Long‐term low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) has hitherto been the standard treatment for cancer patients with VTE. Data comparing LMWH with direct oral anticoagulants in cancer patients with VTE were limited. Aim: The primary aim was to assess VTE recurrence in cancer patients treated with rivaroxaban or dalteparin. Secondary objectives were to assess bleeding and to assess extended anticoagulation beyond 6 months in patients considered at higher risk for recurrent VTE (e.g. compression ultrasound showing residual deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or qualifying event as a pulmonary embolism (PE). Materials and Methods: A randomised open‐label pilot trial was conducted. Cancer patients with symptomatic PE, incidental PE or symptomatic lower extremity proximal DVT were allocated to dalteparin (200 IU/kg daily, month 1, then 150 IU/kg, months 2‐6) or rivaroxaban (15mg twice daily for 3 weeks then 20mg once daily, for 6 months in total). After 6 months of treatment, patients at higher risk of VTE could be randomized to placebo or rivaroxaban for a further 6 months. The primary outcome was VTE recurrence. Secondary outcomes included major bleeds; clinically relevant nonmajor bleeds (CRNMB) and survival. The target sample size was 530 patients. In September 2016, the second randomization was discontinued because of low accrual (n=92) and the trial sample size was reduced to 400 patients, thus providing estimates of VTE recurrence to within +/‐4.5% assuming 10% at 6 months. Results: 406 patients (203 on each arm) were recruited between October 2013 and December 2016 from 58 sites in the United Kingdom. Characteristics of patients were: median age 67 years (range 22‐87); 53% males; 58% with metastases; 48% with symptomatic PE or DVT, and 52% with incidental PE. The 6‐month cumulative VTE recurrence rate was 11% (95% CI 7‐16%) for dalteparin and 4% (95% CI 2‐9%) for rivaroxaban. The 6‐month cumulative rate of major bleeding was 4% (95% CI 2‐8%) for dalteparin and 6% (95% CI 3‐11%) for rivaroxaban. The corresponding rates of CRNMB were 4% (95% CI 2‐9%) and 13% (95% CI 9‐19%). Survival at 6 months was 70% (95% CI 63‐76%) on dalteparin and 75% (95% CI 69‐81%) on rivaroxaban. Conclusions: In terms of therapeutic decision making, a careful discussion should occur between patient and physician concerning the risks of recurrence and bleeding.
