DO  -  10.1002/ajh.24471
AB  -  Background: Venous thromboembolism (VTE) is a common problem within the general population. It has an even higher incidence within the cancer population and VTE significantly increases mortality in cancer patients. LMWH is currently the preferred treatment of cancer associated VTE based on the CLOT trial. Many cancer patients are seeking an oral alternative to daily subcutaneous therapy. Rivaroxaban, a factor Xa inhibitor, may be an appealing oral choice for such patients. Objectives: Our primary objective was to determine the efficacy of rivaroxaban vs. dalteparin in cancer patients for the treatment of VTE. Efficacy was determined by the incidence of recurrent VTE within 30 days, 3 months, and 6 months. Our secondary objective was to determine the safety of rivaroxaban by analyzing major and minor bleeding outcomes in cancer patients compared to dalteparin. Methods: We performed a retrospective chart review of cancer patients > 18 years of age treated at Moffitt Cancer Center between May 3, 2010 and October 5, 2015 with rivaroxaban or dalteparin. 251 patients were identified after applying our exclusion criteria. 152 Dalteparin patients and 99 rivaroxaban patients were analyzed. Patients were excluded that were on anticoagulant therapy duration <30 days, anticoagulant therapy initiated > 6 months after VTE diagnosis, indication for treatment was not DVT/PE, patients on prophylactic doses, and patients with no follow up within 6 months. Major bleeding was defined by standard International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis (ISTH) criteria and any other clinically significant non‐major bleeding was defined as minor bleeding. Recurrent VTE was analyzed within 30 days, 3 months, and 6 months. Descriptive statistical analyses were utilized. Chi square analysis and t‐ test were performed to compare categorical and continuous variables. All data was analyzed using SPSS version 21.0 statistical software. Results: There was no statistically significant difference in baseline characteristics (age, gender, median platelet count, proportion of catheter associated VTE, or stage of cancer) between groups. Four dalteparin patients had recurrent VTE within 30 days compared to 2 rivaroxaban patients (p = 0.76). Five dalteparin patients had recurrent VTE within 3 months vs. 2 rivaroxaban patients (p = 0.79). Nine dalteparin had recurrent VTE within 6 months vs. 4 rivaroxaban patients (p = 0.78). The rivaroxaban group had 3 major and 9 minor bleeding episodes compared to 4 major bleeds and 5 minor bleeding episodes in the dalteparin group with p values of 0.85 and 0.09 respectively. Conclusion: We found no statistical difference in the safety and efficacy of rivaroxaban compared to dalteparin in patients with predominantly active cancer treated at our large comprehensive cancer center. There were no significant differences in regards to recurrent thrombosis at 30 days, 3 months, or 6 months and no difference in major/minor bleeding between patients on rivaroxaban compared to dalteparin in our cohort of patients. However, large multicenter randomized trials evaluating the safety and efficacy of rivaroxaban in the oncology population are needed to further validate our findings.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01732181/full
