DO  -  10.1177/0269216310366390
AB  -  Background: Thromboprophylaxis (TP) for hospice inpatients is contentious. Current guidance is for all patients to be risk‐assessed for venous thromboembolism (VTE) on admission to hospital and TP given accordingly. Application to hospice inpatients is unclear; most have moderate‐high risk for VTE with no likelihood of risk reduction. There is concern about use, whilst recognising that some may benefit. The Pan‐Birmingham Cancer Network (PBCN) guidance aims to identify hospice patients who may benefit from TP with low‐molecular weight heparin (LMWH). Methods: Case notes of 100 consecutive admissions were retrospectively reviewed using the PBCN guidance in 3 UK hospices. We recorded TP, VTE risk, contraindications to anticoagulation and demographic data. Results: Data is presented for 2 hospices. Data from the third has been collected and will be included by end of 2009. 92 men and 108 women had a mean age of 71 (range 22‐96). The diagnosis was cancer in 160. VTE risk: 0 low; 157 moderate; 43 high. 17/200 (8.5%) patients were thought to have a temporary further elevation in risk warranting TP of whom 8 were on LMWH on admission, and 2 started on admission. 22 (11%) were on LMWH/warfarin for secondary prevention. 12/200 (6%) patients had a documented decision regarding TP. TP was contraindicated in 85/200 (42.5%). (Table presented) Conclusions: Most patients did not fulfil PBCN criteria for TP on admission. However, 8.5% had a reversible increased VTE risk. PBCN guideline use is now routine in these 3 hospices and a prospective evaluation is ongoing. However, it is unknown whether such applied guidance, developed by consensus only, is valid; would it be better for hospice inpatients to have TP unless contraindicated? A randomised controlled trial is needed.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01741267/full
