DO  -  10.1016/j.rpth.2023.100915
AB  -  Background: COVID‐19 is associated with adverse outcomes including progression to ICU‐level organ support and death. In the ATTACC/ACTIV‐ 4a/REMAP‐CAP multi‐platform RCT, therapeutic‐dose heparin improved survival without ICU‐level organ support in non‐critically ill patients hospitalized for COVID‐19. The type of heparin administered was pragmatically left to the discretion of investigators at individual sites. Given the molecular differences that exist among heparins, it is unclear if the average treatment effect applies to each heparin type. Aims: To evaluate differential treatment effects due to variation of heparin type. Methods: We evaluated non‐critically ill patients randomized within the ATTACC and ACTIV‐4a platforms. Our primary outcome was a 3‐level ordinal composite of ICU‐level organ support and mortality. Treatment effects were estimated using proportional odds logistic regression within a Bayesian framework. Results:We included 1810 non‐critically ill patients who received heparin on day 1. Therapeutic‐dose heparin was randomly allocated to 979 (54.1%) patients of whom 887 (49.0%) received enoxaparin, 41 (2.3%) dalteparin, 51 (2.8%) unfractionated heparin, and 8 (0.4%) who received other heparin types. The remaining 831 (45.9%) patients were allocated to usual care venous thromboprophylaxis. Therapeutic‐dose enoxaparin likely improved survival without ICU‐level organ support (odds ratio [OR] 1.19, 95% credible interval [CrI] 0.94–1.52; posterior probability [Pr] 92.5%). Due to small sample sizes, inferences were limited for dalteparin (OR 0.72, CrI 0.36–1.50; Pr 17.8%), unfractionated heparin (OR 1.17, CrI 0.57–2.57; Pr 65.4%) and other types of LWMHs. Therapeutic‐dose enoxaparin was associated with increased major bleeding (OR 2.33, CrI 0.87–6.67; Pr 95.3%) and reduced combined thrombosis and death (OR 0.74, CrI 0.51–1.09; PrS 93.5%). Conclusion(s): In non‐critically ill patients hospitalized for COVID‐19, therapeutic‐dose enoxaparin was associated with improved survival without receipt of ICU‐level organ support compared with usual care venous thromboprophylaxis. Infrequent use of other heparins limited potential inferences about treatment effects. [Figure presented]
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02665780/full
