DO  -  10.1016/j.rpth.2023.100295
AB  -  Background: A small subgroup of patients treated for lower leg trauma requiring immobilization develop venous thromboembolism (VTE). The TRiP (cast) score is aimed to predict VTE risk in trauma patients. Aims: The aim was to assess the safety of a strategy to withhold preventive anticoagulation in patients with low VTE risk. Methods: CASTING was a stepped‐wedge cluster‐randomized trial. Fifteen Emergency Departments in France and Belgium were randomly assigned to staggered start dates for switching from the control phase (physician's usual practice) to the intervention phase (targeted anticoagulation according to TRiP(cast) score: no if < 7 and anticoagulation if ≥ 7). The primary outcome was 3‐month cumulative symptomatic VTE rate during the intervention phase in patients with a TRiP(cast) score < 7. The targeted strategy was considered safe if this rate was < 2% (prespecified threshold). Secondary outcomes included overall rate of anticoagulated patients, cumulative 3‐ month VTE incidence, and bleeding rates. Results: Of the 2108 patients analyzed (out of 2120 included), 1159 (55.0%) were deemed at low risk during the interventional phase and received no anticoagulant. Their symptomatic VTE rate was 0.7% (95% confidence interval [CI] 0.3 to 1.4, n = 8/1159). The overall anticoagulation rate was 50.4% (n = 304/603) in the control phase and 24.5% (n = 368/1505) in the interventional phase (absolute difference ‐26.0%; 95% CI ‐30.2 to ‐21.7, p< 0.001). The 3‐month cumulative rate of symptomatic VTE was 1.0% (95%CI 0.2 to 1.8; n = 6/603) in the control phase and 1.1% (95%CI 0.6 to 1.7; n = 17/1505) in the intervention phase (absolute difference 0.1%; 95%CI ‐0.8 to 1.1). Conclusion(s): Targeting thromboprophylaxis on the TRiP(cast) score allowed to safely withhold anticoagulation in a large subgroup of patients at low risk of VTE. Overall anticoagulant prescription was significantly reduced as compared to current practice. [Figure presented]
