AB  -  Background: The current UK venous thromboembolism (VTE) prevention recommendations state that patients who are deemed at medium or high risk for VTE, in whom there are no contraindications, should receive both pharmacologic and mechanical thromboprophylaxis in the form of graduated compression stockings (GCS). The American College of Chest Physicians recommends pharmacologic thromboprophylaxis (Grade 2B) or mechanical thromboprophylaxis, preferably intermittent pneumatic compression (Grade 2C), for moderate‐risk patients but a combination of pharmacologic and mechanical thromboprophylaxis for high‐risk patients (Grade 2C recommendation for the addition of mechanical thromboprophylaxis). The evidence base on which these recommendations were made is weak and has recently been contested. A trial has been designed to clarify this issue. Methods: This is a multicenter, prospective, randomized controlled trial funded by the National Institute for Health Research Health Technology Assessment Programme (project No. 14/140/61).Eligible, consenting adult elective surgical patients identified as being at moderate and high risk for VTE will be randomized to receive either the current “standard” combined low‐dose low‐molecular‐weight heparin (LMWH) with GCS mechanical thromboprophylaxis or low‐dose LMWH pharmacoprophylaxis alone. Telephone or online review will be performed at 1 week after surgery or at discharge and at 90 days. Patients will be referred for imaging if deep venous thrombosis or pulmonary embolism is clinically suspected. Routine bilateral full lower limb duplex ultrasound examination will be performed between 14 days and 21 days postoperatively to capture peak VTE incidence.To show noninferiority (3.5% noninferiority margin) for the primary end point of all VTE within 90 days, 2236 patients are required. Secondary outcomes include quality of life, compliance with stockings and LMWH, overall mortality, and GCS‐ or LMWH‐related complications. Recruitment commenced in April 2016 with a recruitment period of 18 months from seven UK centers. Results:The study primary end point is VTE within 90 days. This is a composite end point including duplex ultrasound‐proven new lower limb deep venous thrombosis up to 90 days after surgery (symptomatic or asymptomatic) plus symptomatic pulmonary embolism (imaging confirmed) up to 90 days after surgery. To date, 833 patients (43% men, 57% women; mean age, 61 years) have been screened from the following specialties: general, 53%; urology, 13%; orthopedics, 12%; otolaryngology, 9%; obstetrics and gynecology, 6%; neurology, 4%; plastics, 2%; and other, 1%. Of these, 160 patients have been randomized, a 19% inclusion rate (47% men, 53% women; mean age, 60 years); 18% of screened patients were ineligible as they would not receive LMWH before discharge, 8% were receiving therapeutic anticoagulation or had a previous VTE, 6% were to receive prolonged thromboprophylaxis, and 23% declined to take part. Conclusions: This study will be the first large randomized multicenter trial to compare VTE outcomes in surgical patients assessed as being at moderate or high risk who are prescribed GCS in addition to low‐dose LMWH and those prescribed low‐dose LMWH alone. The trial will also address issues of complications, adherence, and compliance with GCS and will support future guidance and policy in VTE prevention worldwide.
