DO  -  10.1002/ajh.25268
AB  -  Background: The efficacy and safety or rivaroxaban versus enoxaparin/vitamin K antagonist (VKA) for treatment and prevention of recurrence of venous thromboembolism (VTE) was demonstrated in the randomized EINSTEIN clinical trials. In the pooled EINSTEIN trial population, rivaroxaban was found to be non‐inferior to enoxaparin/VKA in preventing recurrent VTE, while reducing patients' hazard of major bleeding by 46%. Objectives: We sought to compare the effectiveness and safety of rivaroxaban and warfarin in unprovoked VTE patients managed in routine United States (US) clinical practice. Methods: Using claims data from the US Truven MarketScan Commercial and Medicare supplemental databases from January 1, 2012 through December 31, 2016, we identified adults with A primary International Classification of Diseases‐Tenth Revision (ICD‐10) discharge diagnosis of unprovoked deep vein thrombosis (DVT)(ICD‐10=I80.1, I80.2, I80.3, I80.8, I80.9, I81.9, I63.6, I67.6, I82.2, I82.3, I82.9) or pulmonary embolism (PE)(ICD‐10=I26) during A hospitalization or emergency department visit, newly‐initiated on rivaroxaban or warfarin within 30 days after the VTE and with ≥12 months of continuous medical and prescription benefits prior to the VTE (baseline). Patients with provoked VTE (defined as having A history of major surgery, fracture or trauma within 90 days of index VTE event, pregnancy within 42 weeks, A diagnosis of cancer or admitted to hospital for ≥3 consecutive days in the previous 3 months), with A claim for anticoagulation during the baseline period or who redeemed prescriptions for both rivaroxaban and warfarin, or other oral anticoagulants were excluded. Incidences of recurrent VTE and major bleeding (per the Cunningham algorithm) were assessed. Differences in baseline characteristics between cohorts were adjusted for using inverse probability of treatment weights based on propensity scores. Patients were followed post‐index VTE until occurrence of an outcome of interest, insurance disenrollment or end of A 3 or 6 month intention to treat analysis follow‐up. Cox regression was performed to compare rates of outcomes with rivaroxaban versus warfarin and results are reported as hazard ratios (HRs) with 95% confidence intervals (CIs). Results: We identified 59,855 incident VTEs managed with either rivaroxaban or warfarin, of which 36,853 (62%) were deemed unprovoked and constituted our study cohort. Of these, 10,489 were rivaroxaban users and 26,364 warfarin users. Mean±standard deviation age of patients was 57±15 years, 50% were male and 49% had PE±DVT as their index VTE event. At 3 months, rivaroxaban was associated with A significant 39% reduction in recurrent VTE and A 23% reduction in major bleeding hazard versus warfarin (Table). Six months post‐index VTE, rivaroxaban was associated with A significant 40% reduction in recurrent VTE and A 20% reduction in major bleeding versus warfarin. At 3 and 6 months, intracranial hemorrhage and gastrointestinal bleeding was at least numerically lower in rivaroxaban compared to warfarin users. Conclusions: Rivaroxaban appears to reduce unprovoked VTE patients' hazard of both recurrent VTE when used in routine clinical practice. Our results appear consistent with EINSTEIN and post‐marketing registry studies.
