DO  -  10.1016/j.rpth.2023.100360
AB  -  Background: COVID‐19 is associated with progression to ICU‐level organ support and death. The ATTACC/ACTIV‐4a/REMAP‐CAP multi‐platform RCT (mpRCT) demonstrated a benefit of therapeutic‐dose heparin compared with usual care venous thromboprophylaxis in hospitalized, non‐critically ill patients with COVID‐19; therapeutic‐dose heparin was not beneficial in patients requiring ICU‐level organ support at baseline. Whether therapeutic‐dose heparin should be continued in patients who transition to require ICU‐level organ support is unknown. Aims: To examine the impact of therapeutic‐dose heparin on disease state transition in non‐critically ill patients hospitalized for COVID‐19. Methods: We included hospitalized, non‐critically ill patients with COVID‐ 19 enrolled in the ATTACC and ACTIV‐4a platforms. The outcome used 3 irreversible states of COVID‐19 disease severity: non‐critically ill, ICU‐level organ support, and death. We employed a multi‐state model to analyze the treatment effect of heparin on transitions between states. Results: Of the 2095 patients hospitalized for COVID‐19 and enrolled in the ATTACC and ACTIV‐4a platforms, we included 1922 non‐critically ill patients. Heparin was associated with reduced progression from non critically ill to ICU‐level organ support (hazard ratio [HR] 0.84, 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.68 to 1.02; p = 0.08). Therapeutic‐dose heparin was not associated with reduced mortality among patients in the non critically ill state (HR 1.26, CI 0.64 to 2.49; p = 0.50), or in those who had transitioned to require organ support (HR 0.91, CI 0.62 to 1.34; p = 0.64). Patients requiring organ support experienced higher rates of bleeding and thrombotic events compared to those who were not critically ill. Conclusion(s): Compared to usual care venous thromboprophylaxis, therapeutic‐dose heparin may be associated with reduced progression to ICU‐level organ support in hospitalized, non‐critically ill patients with COVID‐19. Therapeutic‐dose heparin did not reduce mortality in patients who were non‐critically ill or in those that transitioned to require ICU‐level organ support. Patients on ICU‐level organ support experienced higher rates of bleeding and thrombosis. [Figure presented]
