AB  -  Background: COVID‐19 causes a spectrum of disease ranging from asymptomatic infection to critical illness and death. In the ATTACC/ ACTIV‐4a/REMAP‐CAP multi‐platform RCT, therapeutic‐dose heparin improved survival without ICU‐level organ support in non‐critically ill patients hospitalized for COVID‐19. It is unknown whether patients who had COVID‐19 symptoms for longer before hospitalization are at greater risk of progression to ICU‐level organ support or death, or if there is a differential effect of therapeutic‐dose heparin by symptom‐days. Aims: To explore the impact of COVID‐19 symptom‐days prior to hospitalization on survival without ICU‐level organ support and the impact of symptom‐days on the treatment effect of therapeutic‐dose heparin. Methods: We included non‐critically ill patients randomized in the ATTACC platform. Our primary outcome was a 3‐level composite of ICU‐level organ support and mortality. Effects were estimated using a proportional odds logistic regression within a Bayesian framework. The effect of symptom‐days on therapeutic‐dose heparin was evaluated using an interaction term. Results:We evaluated 1148 hospitalized, non‐critically ill patients of whom 1137 had date of first COVID‐19 symptom. Therapeutic‐dose heparin and usual care venous thromboprophylaxis were allocated to 638 (56.1%) and 499 (43.9%) patients, respectively. Each additional day of symptoms prior to hospital admission was associated with improved survival without ICU‐level organ support (odds ratio [OR] 1.08, 95% credible interval [CrI] 1.04–1.13; posterior probability [Pr] >99.9%). Additional symptom‐days were associated with reductions in intubation (Pr 99.5%), mortality at 90 days (Pr 99.5%), combined total thrombosis and death (Pr 98.2%), an increase in hospital‐free days (Pr 99.9%), as well as a minor impact on major bleeding (Pr 74.5%). Symptom‐days did not modify the treatment effect of the rapeuticdose heparin (OR for interaction 0.98, CrI 0.90–1.05; Pr 27.2%). Conclusion(s): Days of COVID‐19 symptoms prior to hospitalization were strongly associated with improved survival without ICU‐level organ support and did not modify the treatment effect of therapeutic‐dose heparin. [Figure presented]
