AB  -  Background: The incidence of venous thromboembolism (VTE) is increased in cancer patients compared to the general population. Treatment of cancer‐associated VTE is difficult and long‐term LMWH therapy has been shown superior to conventional treatment with warfarin. However, in spite of adequate anticoagulation with LMWH, a 9‐10% risk of thrombotic recurrences (Schulman et al, JTH 2015) still remains in these patients, with an increased bleeding risk compared to non‐cancer patients. Aim of this prospective study was to evaluate whether and to what extent LMWH is capable to modulate clotting activation in these patients and establish whether any of the activation markers included may predict for VTE recurrences and/or hemorrhagic complications. Methods: Plasma samples were obtained from 62 consecutive cancer patients with acute VTE. Blood samples were collected at the thrombotic event, before starting LMWH treatment (T0), at one month of LMWH therapy (T1), and at discontinuation of LMWH, i.e. after 6 months from VTE (T2). The following parameters were evaluated: 1. thrombin generation (TG), a global hemostatic assay, assessed by the CAT assay; 2. microparticle (MP)‐associated procoagulant activity (PCA) assessed by the STA Procoag PPL assay; and 3. D‐dimer levels measured with HemosIL D‐dimer test. Patients were followed up for symptomatic thrombotic and bleeding events. results At T0 patients were characterized by a procoagulant phenotype as reflected by increased TG, MP‐associated PCA, and D‐dimer levels compared to controls. During LMWH, a significant reduction in both TG potential and D‐dimer levels was observed. After LMWH discontinuation (T2), TG potential as well as D‐dimer levels increased compared to T1, becoming similar to that of controls. Differently, no variation in MP‐PCA occurred. In this cohort, none of the patients had a VTE recurrence. One major bleeding episode occurred. Twenty‐nine patients (47%) died during follow‐up because of disease progression. High D‐dimer level at enrolment predicted for reduced survival rate (HR=3.69). Conclusions: This study demonstrates that LMWH treatment modulates the global prothrombotic potential in cancer patients, through the inhibition of TG. This effect is accompanied by the normalization of the hypercoagulable status, as indicated by the reduction in D‐dimer levels. No thrombotic recurrences were observed, suggesting that LMWH treatment was effective. Given this circumstance it was not possible to calculate the predictive value for VTE of any of the measured parameters. These data point to the TG assay as a possible marker to monitor global anticoagulant effect of LMWH in cancer patients.
