DO  -  10.1016/j.jvsv.2021.12.034
AB  -  Objective: Chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension (CTEPH) is a morbid complication following acute pulmonary embolism (PE). The optimal treatment to prevent this sequela remains unknown. We aimed to compare the biomarker profile and short‐term functional outcomes in patients who received catheter‐directed thrombolysis (CDT) versus anticoagulation alone. Methods: This is a secondary analysis of the SUNSET sPE randomized trial comparing standard catheter directed thrombolysis to ultrasound assisted thrombolysis in patients presenting with submassive PE. As part of this study, patients who did not receive an intervention were enrolled in a medical (anticoagulation only) arm. Blood was collected from each of the study participants and biomarkers that have been associated with CTEPH in the literature (CCL2/MCP‐1, CXCL10/CRG‐2, pentraxin 3/TSG‐14, GDF‐15, RAGE/AGER, CXCL13/BLC/BCA‐1) were measured at diagnosis, at discharge, and at three‐month follow‐up. All patients underwent a 6‐minute walk test and answered quality of life questionnaires (PEmb, UCSD health, SF36) 3 months after diagnosis. Comparisons were made using the Student t test. Nonparametric tests were used when the distributions were not normal. Significance was set at a P value of less than or equal to.05. Results: Seventy‐two patients (56 ± 15 years; 40.3% female) were included in the analysis. Fifty‐three of these patients underwent CDT as part of the SUNSET sPE trial. Nineteen additional patients were included in the medical arm of the study (heparin transitioned to apixaban, rivaroxaban, or warfarin). Baseline RV/LV ratios were similar between groups (ratio of 1.8 in CDT group, 1.7 in medical group). Mortality, adverse events, and hospital stay were similar between groups. At time of discharge, CXCL10/CRG‐2 (P =.04) and Pentraxin 3/TSG‐14 (P =.03) were lower in the CDT group, and the difference normalized by 3‐month follow‐up (Fig 1, A, B). This trend, while not significant, was seen in most of the other biomarkers as well (Fig 1, C‐E). Oxygen requirements at time of discharge and at three‐month follow‐up were 15.1% and 0% for CDT group versus 26.3% and 5.3% for anticoagulation group (P =.31). At 3 months, 6‐minute walking distance (Fig 2, A) and quality of life scores (Fig 2, B) were similar between the two groups. Conclusions: In patients with submassive PE, biomarkers of CTEPH normalize earlier with catheter thrombolysis compared to anticoagulation alone. At 3 months both groups demonstrated similar biomarker levels, 6‐minute walking distance, and qualitative metrics. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
