AB  -  Background: Background: Cancer is a hypercoagulable state associated with estimated increase in the risk of venous thrombembolism (VTE) of 4 to 7 times the baseline risk of the general population. The most common type of cancers, prostate in men and breast in women, contributed the most to the overall burden of cancer associated thrombosis (CAT). The highest VTE incidence rate was found in patients with pancreatic cancer, following after it are brain and ovarian cancer. CAT was associated with overall mortality rate of 67,7 % (95 %CI, 65,9‐69,7) per 100 person years and the second leading cause of death in cancer patients receiving chemotherapy. Aims: METHODS AND RESULTS: Results were extrapolated for newly diagnosed patients with VTE from computer program trombo. net in the anticoagulation ambulance in Slovenj Gradec, making comparison of results in the years 2018 and 2019. Patients were divided in groups based on diagnosis‐with pulmonary embolism, venous thrombosis of other sites or both, cancer type (gastrointestinal, hematological or others), anticoagulation therapy (low molecular weight heparin LMWH), antagonists of vitamin K, direct Xa inhibitors and thrombin antagonist DOACs), complications‐bleedings and recurrence of VTE. Methods: We analyzed 113 patients in two years 2018‐2019, 57 men with average age of 72,5 y and 56 women with average age of 71 . 54 pts had pulmonary embolism, 33 proximal or distal thrombosis/other locations and 26 patients had both. Therapy: 65 patients on LMWH, peroral antagonist of vitamin K ‐17 pts in DOAC s‐30 pts. In the study 22 patients had bleedings‐12 minor and 10major and recurrence of VTE while on therapy‐3. During the study 56 patients died. Results: Discusion: Complication of anticoagulation therapy‐10 major bleedings on LMWH therapy and antagonist of vitamin K (8,8 %), 12 minor bleedings on DOACs (10,6 %). During the study in the first year of therapy 49,5 % of the patients died. From literature the study SELECT‐D randomized, open label, multicenter pilot study in 2018‐400 patients with CAT, compared rivaroxaban with dalteparin (LMWH). The study's primary outcome was recurrence of VTE during 6‐month follow up therapy. The cumulative incidence of VTE was 4 % in the rivaroxaban group compared with 11 % in the LMWH group. The 6‐month cummulative incidence of major bleeding was 6 % in patients on rivaroxaban and 4 % on LMWH, the patients with esophageal and gastric cancer were excluded because of risk of bleeding, more bleeding events were also in urologic cancers. Compared with these results our patients had more bleeding rate, but patients with all cancers were included (breast, prostate, pulmonal, gastrointestinal, melanoma, gynecological and hematological). We have less VTE recurrence rate‐only 2,6 %. Summary/Conclusion: Conclusion: Guidelines ESC (European society of cardiology) in 2019, ISTH 2018 and experts on education session on ASH Congress 2019, Orlando, USA currently recommend the use of low molecular weight heparin for cancer associated thrombosis (grade IIA). The same grade IIA recommendation is for direct Xa inhibitors for therapy of CAT, bleeding risk factors must be excluded and it is not for patients with luminal gastrointestinal and urothelial cancer and if it is drug to drug interactions. When starting Xa inhibitors the appropriate combination must be re‐assessed after every 3 months of therapy and we should consider extending the therapy beyond 6 months, if thecancer remains active .(lecture of Miriam Kripton and Marc Carrier ASH 2019).
