DO  -  10.1097/01.JU.0001016880.88141.73
AB  -  Venous thromboembolism (VTE) constitutes a significant burden in terms of morbidity, mortality, and cost in patients with bladder cancer, especially those undergoing radical cystectomy (RC) with pelvic lymph node dissection (PLND). Current literature supports the use of extended prophylaxis (EP) with enoxaparin through 28 days post‐op, showing EP is associated with almost half the rate of VTE in the immediate post op period. Even as multiple guidelines endorse some form of extended prophylaxis, universal adoption remains elusive due to barriers such as high cost to the patient, trouble with adherence and difficulty associated with enoxaparin administration. Direct oral anticoagulants such as apixaban present an attractive alternative to enoxaparin injections. A recent trial in a gynecologic oncology population randomized patients to apixaban or enoxaparin for VTE prophylaxis postop and reported increased ease of administration and decreased pain in the apixaban group, with no difference in VTE rates noted between the two groups (Guntupali et al., JAMA 2020). There is also retrospective data from single‐institutions using apixaban after cystectomy which report no increased rate of VTE (Westerman et al., J Urol 2022). While encouraging, there has yet to be a prospective, randomized trial comparing the practical patient experience with these two forms of VTE prophylaxis to determine if apixaban can be an acceptable alternative to enoxaparin for EP after radical cystectomy. This trial is a multi‐center, randomized, open‐blinded, pragmatic clinical trial designed to compare adherence, satisfaction, patient outof‐ pocket cost, and VTE rates between discharge on apixaban vs enoxaparin as EP after RC. Randomization is 1:1, stratified by site. Study Population: To be included, patients must undergo a RC with PLND for urothelial cancer and be medically eligible to go home on a prophylactic dose of either anticoagulant. This notably excludes patients already on therapeutic doses of anticoagulant for other medical reasons and those who cannot take enoxaparin due to dialysis. Patients must be discharged by post op day (POD) 14 to remain in the trial. Protocol: Patients will be screened, enrolled, and randomized preoperatively. There is no impact of trial participation on a participant's surgery or inpatient recovery. At the time of discharge participants will be given a script for the appropriate medication that they must fill themselves. They will be instructed to take the medication through POD 30. The trial protocol includes two study visits, both completed via phone call: one shortly after POD 30 and the second at POD 90. The study is complete after the POD 90 phone call. Endpoints: The primary endpoint of the trial is participant adherence, measured as the proportion of days covered (PDC). The PDC is calculated as the number of days the patient took their medication out of the number of days they were instructed to take it. The PDC will be determined by completing medication counting with participants during their first study phone call. Secondary endpoints include patient out‐of‐pocket cost for the drug, patient satisfaction and reasons for non‐adherence, and VTE and bleeding events. Out‐of‐pocket cost will be measured through query of institutional pharmacy data. VTE and bleeding events will be detected by phone screening with chart review and referral to the treating clinician as needed. Satisfaction and reasons for nonadherence will be collected using both validated and novel questionnaires administered via phone and email. Validated questionnaires include the National Institute of Health (NIH) Patient‐Reported Outcomes Measurement Information System (PROMIS) Medication Adherence Scale (PMAS) and the Comprehensive Score for Financial ToxicityeFunctional Assessment of Chronic Illness Therapy (COST‐FACIT). Statistical Analysis: Apixaban will first be evaluated for noninferiority compared to enoxaparin with respect to the primary endpoint, the PDC (HA: the PDC of apixaban is non‐inferior to that of enoxaparin). The margin for noninferiority is a 15% difference in PDC, which translates to approximately 3 days of adherence. For 80% power on a one‐sided p test, the target sample size is 90 total patients. Testing for superiority (HA: PDC of apixaban is superior to that of enoxaparin) will be performed if non‐inferiority is found. Descriptive analyses of the secondary endpoints of cost, satisfaction, and reasons for nonadherence will be performed. VTE and bleeding rates will be reported although the trial is not powered to formally test for differences between the study arms. Enrollment Status: The CARE trial received approval from the University of Pennsylvania Institutional Review Board and is registered at ClinicalTrials.gov. The trial is currently enrolling subjects at the University of Pennsylvania, undergoing IRB review to begin enrolling at Vanderbilt University, and open to the continued addition of participating sites.
