DO  -  10.1177/1747493018789543
AB  -  Background: To prevent recurrent venous thrombotic events (VTE) after acute cerebral venous or dural sinus thrombosis (CVT), guidelines recommend long‐term oral anticoagulation with vitamin K antagonists. Experience with non‐vitamin‐K oral anticoagulants in CVT is limited to case reports. Aim. To compare dabigatran with dose‐adjusted warfarin in patients with CVT for the prevention of recurrent VTE. Methods. RE‐SPECT CVT was a phase III, prospective, randomized, parallel‐group, openlabel, multicenter, exploratory trial with blinded endpoint adjudication (ClinicalTrials.gov number: NCT02913326). Patients with acute CVT were eligible for randomization after initial treatment with parenteral heparin for 5‐15‐days. Patients were randomized (1:1) to either dabigatran etexilate 150 mg twice daily or dose‐adjusted (INR 2‐3) warfarin (up to24‐weeks). The primary endpoint was a composite of the number of patients with new VTE (recurring CVT, deep venous thrombosis of any other vein, pulmonary embolism) or major bleeding (International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis definition) during the study period. Results. Inclusion started in December 2016 and was completed in December 2017. 120 patients were included from 36 active centers in 9 countries. The final results will be presented at the WSC 2018Discussion. This exploratory trial is the first to compare vitamin‐K with non‐vitamin‐K antagonist in CVT. It will provide evidence to guide physicians and patients in choosing oral anticoagulants to prevent VTE after acute CVT.
