DO  -  10.1016/j.rpth.2023.100660
AB  -  Background: In patients with intermediate‐risk pulmonary embolism (PE), reversal of hypoxic vasoconstriction could constitute a target for treatment. Aims: The AIR trial aimed to assess the effect of oxygen in patients with intermediate‐risk PE who did not have hypoxemia at baseline. Methods: AIR randomly assigned non‐hypoxemic patients who had stable PE associated with echocardiographic right ventricle (RV) enlargement to receive anticoagulation plus supplemental oxygen at a concentration of 35% (7 l/min) continuously for the first 48 hours versus ambient air plus anticoagulation. The primary outcome was normal RV size on echocardiography measured 48 hours after randomization. Secondary efficacy outcomes were the numerical change in the RV/left ventricle (LV) diameter ratio measured 48 hours and 7 days after randomization, with respect to the baseline ratio measured at inclusion. Results: The study was prematurely stopped due to the COVID‐19 pandemic after recruiting 70 patients (mean [SD] age, 67.3 [16.1] years; 36 women [51.4%]). The intention‐to‐treat analysis showed normal RV size at 48 hours in 14 (42.4%) of the 33 patients randomly assigned to oxygen, and in 8 (21.6%) of the 37 patients randomly assigned to ambient air (P = 0.08). In the oxygen group, the mean RV/LV ratio was reduced from 1.28 +/‐ 0.28 at baseline to 1.01 +/‐ 0.16 at 48 hours (P < 0.001); in the ambient air group, mean RV/LV ratios were 1.21 +/‐ 0.18 and 1.08 +/‐ 0.19, respectively (P < 0.01). The mean decrease in RV/LV ratio from baseline to 48 hours was 0.28 +/‐ 0.26 versus 0.12 +/‐ 0.24 (P = 0.02). At 30 days, there was 1 major bleeding (in the ambient air group), no deaths, and no recurrent venous thromboembolism. Conclusion(s): This trial of patients with non‐hypoxemic intermediate‐risk PE showed improvement in some efficacy outcomes for supplemental oxygen compared with ambient air and provided support for a definitive clinical outcomes trial.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02665770/full
