AB  -  Background: Colorectal cancer (CRC) is the third most commonly diagnosed cancer worldwide. CRC leads to increased activation of the clotting system and the propensity to develop venous thromboembolism (VTE) may be partially heritable. Over 50% of the variation in VTE susceptibility is thought to be explained by genetic determinants. The incidence of VTE in patients with CRC has been reported to be approximately 10%, especially in patients with advanced disease. Aims: Evaluate low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) versus observation for primary thromboprophylaxis in metastatic CRC outpatients who are receiving first line therapy. Methods: PROTINCOL (NCT05625932) is a randomized, open‐label, multicenter study (figure 1). Patients will receive tinzaparin (75 IU/kg) or no treatment and they will be stratified based on: BRAF/RAS mutation, primary resection tumour and antiangiogenic therapy. The study outcomes will be assessed by a blind central independent adjudication committee. The primary efficacy endpoints will be the cumulative incidence of any VTE event (symptomatic or incidental) including symptomatic central venous catheter VTE. Secondary variables: clinically relevant bleedings as well as healthrelated quality of life and risk assessment of VTE based on a validated clinical‐genetic model. Our hypothesis is that prophylactic LMWH will reduce 55% relative risk from an estimated VTE incidence of 13.5%. A total of 526 patients will be required. This study has received the independent ethics committee approval. Results: They are expected by the end of 2024. Conclusion(s): We selected a LMWH instead of a direct oral anticoagulant because CRC patients represent a high risk group for bleeding. For the first time in ambulatory prophylaxis of cancer‐associated thrombosis, a precision medicine approach will be used in a randomized clinical trial. If the individualization of antithrombotic prophylaxis can reduce the complications of outpatient cancer treatment and be cost effective, it would be of great value in the future care of patients with metastatic CRC. [Figure presented]
