AB  -  Continuous heparin (CH) therapy has been recommended for thromboembolism and is said to have fewer bleeding complications than intermittent heparin (IH). Intravenous CH and IH were compared in 36 patients (pt) with pulmonary embolism (PE). Pt were randomly assigned with 18 in each treatment group; age, sex and severity of disease were similar. Fibrinogen/fibrin degradation products (FDP/fdp) were measured by a tanned red cell hemagglutination inhibition immunoassay, and soluble fibrin complexes (SFC) were measured by a serial dilution protamine sulfate test; these determinations were performed daily for the first 7 days and biweekly thereafter. The overall incidence of bleeding complications was 33% during CH and 28% during IH. Major bleeding (requiring cessation of heparin) occurred in 17% of cases in both treatment groups; minor bleeding (of lesser severity) occurred in 17% with CH and 11% with IH. Two pt had recurrent thromboembolism in the IH group (11%) and 4 in the CH group (22%). None of these differences is statistically significant. There were no differences in rate or degree of normalization of FDP/fdp or SFC in the 2 groups; mean values for both tests were not statistically different on any day of treatment. Therapeutic prolongation of Lee White clotting times was achieved with equal frequency. In pt without overt bleeding, CH was associated with a significantly (p<0.005) greater mean fall in hematocrit (7.9%) than occurred in IH (2.2%). Our data indicate no significant difference in the incidence of bleeding or recurrent thromboembolism with CH and IH. FDP/fdp and SFC show a similar response to either type of therapy. Because CH requires special apparatus for safe administration (e.g., an infusion pump), there may be problems unique to its use that do not occur with IH. Moreover, CH may be associated with an important degree of occult blood loss as reflected in a greater fall in hematocrit. Therefore, due to considerations of convenience and safety, IH is the preferred treatment for most pt with PE.
