CC  -  Vascular
SP  -  365
M3  -  Journal article
AB  -  Background and aims: Patients with cerebral venous thrombosis (CVT) are commonly treated with anticoagulants for 3‐12 months to prevent recurrence of venous thrombotic events. Until recently, vitamin K antagonists (VKA) were the oral anticoagulant of choice in patients with CVT. Direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) are more practical in use than VKA and carry a lower risk of intracranial haemorrhage in other conditions. An exploratory randomized controlled trial including 120 patients with CVT suggested that dabigatran has a similar efficacy and safety profile as VKA in CVT. Data from a real‐world setting are warranted to confirm those findings. Method(s): DOAC‐CVT is an international, prospective, comparative cohort study. Patients are eligible if they are >18 years old, have a radiologically confirmed CVT, and have started oral anticoagulant treatment (DOAC or VKA) within 30 days of CVT diagnosis. The primary endpoint is a composite of major bleeding and symptomatic recurrent venous thrombosis at 6 months of follow‐up. Anticipating a 3:2 ratio in DOAC:VKA use, we aim to recruit a sample size of 500 patients. Result(s): As of January 2022, 162 patients have been included from 17 participating centres across 8 countries. Recruitment is ongoing. Researchers with an interest in joining DOAC‐CVT may contact the coordinating investigator at j.coutinho@amsterdamumc.nl Conclusion(s): DOAC‐CVT will provide real‐world data on the comparative efficacy and safety of DOACs vs. VKA for prevention of recurrence of venous thrombotic events after CVT.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02494362/full
