DO  -  10.1016/j.jvs.2015.04.410
AB  -  It would be feasible to perform a definitive studyregarding the value of therapeutic anticoagulation in patients with isolatedcalf‐vein thrombosis. Therapeutic anticoagulation for lower extremity VTEcarries a quantifiable and significant risk. As a result, there are many instanceswhere aggressive treatment may be counterbalanced by potentialharm. Isolated calf‐vein thrombosis is one of these areas. There has beenonly one prospective randomized trial comparing oral anticoagulationagainst conservative management in isolated calf‐vein thrombosis that reportedan absolute risk reduction of recurrence of 29% with the use of anticoagulation(Lagerstedt C, et al. Lancet 1985;2(8467):1311‐1312).However there are also additional studies that suggest a lower risk inconservatively managed patients with isolated calf vein thrombosis(Schwarz T, et al. J Vasc Surg 2010;52:1246‐1250 and Palareti G et al,Thromb Haemost 2010;104:1063‐70). The American College of ChestPhysicians has adjusted recommendations in current guidelines to potentiallysupport surveillance rather than anticoagulation treatment in lowriskpatients with isolated calf‐vein thrombosis (Guyatt GH, Chest2012;141(2 Suppl):7S‐47S). The authors contend that the controversyis of such significance that a prospective randomized trial evaluating thetreatment of isolated calf‐vein thrombosis should be performed. This currentstudy sought to establish the feasibility of a definitive trial. Specific objectiveswere to define the incidence of isolated calf‐vein thrombosis inambulatory patients and to evaluate recruitment and compliance to a potentialtrial protocol. They also sought to assess clinically relevantcomplication rates in patients with or without anticoagulation. This wasan open‐label, external pilot randomized control trial of consecutive symptomaticambulatory patients with isolated calf‐vein thrombosis. Participantswere approached for inclusion and were allocated to receive eithertherapeutic anticoagulation or conservative management. Patients underwentblinded color‐duplex imaging at 7 and 21 days and clinical followupat 3 months. Principal outcomes included recruitment rate andattrition. Principle clinical outcome was a composite that included proximalpropagation, pulmonary embolism, death attributable to VTE disease,or major bleeding. Analysis was by intention to treat. There were93 patients with isolated calf‐vein thrombosis screened. Of these, 70were eligible and recruited (88.6%). All patients but one were followedupby direct contact after 90 days. Allocation crossover occurred in 15 patients(21.4%). The principle clinical outcome occurred in 4 of 35 of thosetreated conservatively (11.4%) and in zero of 35 receiving anticoagulation(absolute risk reduction, 11.4%; 95% confidence interval, 1.5 to 26.7;P.11). Number needed to treat to avoid a principle clinical outcomewas 9. There were no major bleeding episodes.Comment: The data suggests a definitive trial to assess the benefits oftherapeutic anticoagulation in the management of isolated calf‐vein thrombosiswould be acceptable to patients and be feasible in a modern healthcareenvironment. Given the controversy in this field, one must agree with theauthors thata definitive trial is urgently needed, to provide a concise andgeneralizable estimate of the clinical benefit and cost‐effectiveness seenwith therapeutic anticoagulation in treatment of isolated calf‐vein thrombosis.However, such a trial would have to incorporate multiple subgroupanalyses as isolated calf‐vein thrombosis occurs in a wide variety of patientsand clinical situations.
