DO  -  10.1161/circ.140.suppl_1.14055
AB  -  Introduction: Post pulmonary embolism (PE) syndrome or decrease in functional capacity, occurs in 45‐50% of survivors of PE. Exercise therapy has been advocated to improve functional capacity, but physicians are reluctant to recommend exercise due to safety concerns. Hypothesis: The aim of our study is to assess safety and effectiveness of exercise after PE. Methods: We present an interim analysis of a prospective study using exercise in patients with acute PE and DVT. Unstable patients, those with a contraindication to anticoagulation, or pregnant were excluded. Exercise consisted of 3 months of aerobic and resistance exercises. Adverse events included recurrent PE or DVT, major cardiovascular events and death. Functional capacity was assessed using the Corridor Walk test to quantify walking time, and to estimate maximal aerobic capacity (VO2max). SF‐36 evaluated quality of life (QoL). Subjects were studied at baseline, 3 and 12 months. Results: A total of 23 patients were enrolled, 5 massive, 16 submassive and 2 low risk PE. Mean age was 49.2 ± 12.9 yr, 43% were female and 52% Caucasian. Mean BMI was 33.07 ± 9.35 kg/m2, hypertension was present in 50%, 27.2% had a recent surgery and 17.9% cancer. At 3 and 12 months distance walked in 2min (+9.8 and +16.4m), time required to walk 400 m (‐1.2 and ‐1.8 min), and VO2max(+2.5 p=0.026 and +2.8 p=0.046) improved. Score of SF‐36 for QoL improved at 12 months. There were no deaths, recurrent PE, DVT, or major adverse cardiovascular events. Conclusions: Exercise therapy can be implemented safely after PE. Improvement in functional capacity may be related to intervention or natural history of the disease. Further randomized control trials are needed.
