AB  -  Venous thromboembolism (VTE) is a public health challenge and a major cause of morbidity and mortalily. The Hokusai‐VTE study is a randomized double‐blind study that evaluated whether heparin (5 days) followed by an oral factor Xa inhibitor, Edoxaban, at a dose of 60 mg/day is an alternative to conventional treatment (heparin (5 days) followed by warfarin (target INR between 2 and 3)) for the prevention of recurrence in patients with acute VTE. This study demonstrated in patients with VTE, Including a large subgroup with severe pulmonary embolism and right ventricular dysfunction, non‐inferiority of heparin therapy (5 days) followed by Edoxaban 60 mg/day compared to standard treatment. In terms of safely, less bleeding episodes were recorded in the Heparin‐Edoxaban, at least for fatal and intracranial bleedings while there was no statistically significant difference for major bleeding complications. Half‐dose of Edoxaban (30 mg/day) in patients at increased risk of bleeding (renal failure, low body‐weight, co‐medlcation with agents that inhibit P‐glycoproteln which is a substrate Edoxaban) proved to be safe and effective.
