DO  -  10.1002/ajh.26841
AB  -  Background The SARS‐COV‐2 (COVID‐19) infection triggers a hypercoagulable state causing microvascular and macrovascular thrombosis. A retrospective study in China showed that critically ill patients with markedly elevated D‐Dimers who were on heparin or low‐molecular weight heparin (LMWH) prophylactic‐dose had lower mortality compared to those who did not receive pharmacologic thromboprophylaxis. Whether therapeutic‐dose anticoagulation improves outcomes in acutely ill or critically ill COVID‐19 patients has been investigated in randomized control trials. In our academic institution we designed the IMPACT trial: a prospective phase IV, open‐label randomized control trial comparing two different doses of parenteral anticoagulation in hospitalized patients with COVID‐19 infection. Aims Primary aim: To determine if therapeutic dose anticoagulation improves 30‐day mortality in COVID‐19 positive patients compared to intermediate dose prophylaxis. Secondary aims: 1. To assess if therapeutic anticoagulation reduced the length of ICU stay. 2. To determine if therapeutic anticoagulation reduces the number of documented thrombosis. 3. To evaluate if therapeutic anticoagulation leads to increase major and clinically relevant nonmajor bleeding events. Methods Prospective phase IV, open‐label randomized control trial comparing parenteral anticoagulation (Intermediate vs. therapeutic dose) in hospitalized patients with COVID‐19 infection. Eligibility criteria: requirement of supplemental oxygen of > 4 liters of O2 and a D‐dimer > 3 the upper limit of normal. Exclusion criteria: indication for therapeutic anticoagulation, active bleeding and do not resuscitate status. Patients randomized to the therapeutic arm had a peak anti‐Xa level measured after the 3rd dose targeting anti‐Xa level 0.5‐1.0 IU/mL. Demographics, clinical and laboratory data and outcomes (30‐day mortality, thrombotic and bleeding events) were collected. At discharge patients were assigned to pharmacologic thromboprophylaxis according to hospital guidelines and followed for 6 months. Results From August 2020 to June 2021, a total of 552 patients were screened. Thirty‐three were eligible (18 declined participation and 1 became ineligible after signing consent). Fourteen patients completed the trial. Baseline patient characteristics (Table 1). Mean age was 65.5 years, with male predominance and majority White. Main comorbidities were hyperlipidemia and hypertension. The most common respiratory support was high flow nasal cannula. Laboratory data at screening (Table 2). Mean D‐dimer was > 10 x ULN and there was marked elevation of FVIII, VWF, and fibrinogen. Four patients had positive Lupus anticoagulant. One patient had decreased APC resistance. Anti‐Xa level peak was in therapeutic range for patients in therapeutic arm. Patients in therapeutic arm had significantly longer ICU stays. One patient suffered a pulmonary embolism while on intermediate dose. Patient was transitioned to a therapeutic dose and suffered a major bleeding event. Anticoagulation was stopped and patient had a stroke. Another patient on therapeutic dose anticoagulation had a major bleeding. The mortality was higher in patients on therapeutic dose anticoagulation. Conclusion In our study, the administration of therapeutic dose anticoagulation in hospitalized patients with COVID‐19 did not improve 30‐day mortality. It appeared to increase ICU stay and bleeding events without preventing thrombotic episodes. (Table Presented).
