AB  -  Background: Observational studies indicate that statins reduce therisk of recurrent venous thrombosis (VT), without increasing the riskof bleeding complications. However, trials have not been performedand the mechanism is unknown.Aims: To determine the effect of rosuvastatin on coagulation factorsin patients with VT.Methods: Randomized clinical trial (NCT01613794). Patients withprior VT, who recently discontinued anticoagulant therapy, were randomized to rosuvastatin 20 mg/day for 4 weeks or no intervention.Blood was drawn at baseline and at end of study. The primary outcome (for which the study was powered) was factor (F) VIII:C. In total5 coagulation factors were measured: FVIII:C, von Willebrand factor(VWF:Ag), FVII:C, FXI:C and D‐dimer.Results: Among 247 randomized participants, mean age was 58 years,62% were women, and 49% had unprovoked VT. For all tested coagulation factors, the mean difference in change was lower or tendedto be lower at end of study in rosuvastatin users versus non‐users.Results were most consistent for FVIII:C. In rosuvastatin users, meanFVIII:C levels were 7.2 IU/dL (95% CI,‐11.5 to‐2.9) lower, whileamong non‐users, no change in FVIII:C was observed (mean difference‐0.1; 95% CI,‐3.0 to 2.9). The mean age and sex adjusted difference in FVIII:C change was‐6.7 IU/dL (‐12.0 to‐1.4) in rosuvastatinusers versus non‐users. Out of 52 rosuvastatin users (41%) who hadhigh (>150 IU/dL) FVIII:C levels at baseline, this had normalized in 15participants at end of study; a decrease of 12% (0.1 to 23). In contrast,the percentage of participants with high FVIII:C levels in non‐usersremained the same at end of study compared with baseline.Conclusions: Rosuvastatin 20 mg/day improved the coagulation profile among patients with prior VT. These results encourage conducting a randomized trial to determine whether statin therapy is a goodalternative for long term anticoagulant treatment when it reduces therisk of recurrent VT without inducing bleeding.
