AB  -  Background And Aims: Anticoagulant treatment of pediatric cerebral venous thrombosis (CVT) has not been prospectively evaluated. In the EINSTEIN‐Jr. trial, 500 children with acute venous thrombosis were randomized to open‐label rivaroxaban or standard anticoagulants. We performed a predefined subgroup analysis of the children with CVT. Methods: Children with CVT (aged birth‐17 years) treated at 51 hospitals in 20 countries were randomized (2:1) to rivaroxaban or comparator (heparin or vitamin K antagonist). The treatment period was 3 months. The primary efficacy outcome, symptomatic recurrent thrombosis (assessed by intention‐to‐treat) and the principal safety outcome, major or clinically relevant non‐major bleeding (assessed in children who received ≥ 1 dose) were centrally evaluated by blinded investigators. Functional dependency (pediatric modified Rankin Scale ≥3) and sinus recanalization on repeat brain imaging after 3 months were secondary outcomes. Statistical analyses were exploratory. This trial is registered with ClinicalTrials.gov, #NCT02234843. Results: From March 2015 through Nov 2018, 114 children with confirmed CVT were randomized. None of the 73 rivaroxaban‐recipients and 1 (2.4%; CVT) of the 41 comparator‐recipients had recurrent thrombosis (absolute difference 2.4%; 95%CI ‐2.6‐13.5%). Clinically relevant bleeding occurred in 5 (6.8%; all non‐major) rivaroxaban‐recipients and 1 (2.5%; major [subdural] bleeding) comparatorrecipient (absolute difference 4.4%; 95%CI ‐6.7%‐13.4%). Complete or partial sinus recanalization occurred in 57 (78%) rivaroxaban‐recipients and 30 (73%) comparator‐recipients, respectively. After 3 months, 4 (5.5%) rivaroxaban‐recipients and 3 (7.3%) comparator‐recipients were functionally dependent, respectively. Conclusions: Most children with CVT treated with either rivaroxaban or standard anticoagulation had a favorable outcome, with low risks of recurrent thrombosis and clinically relevant bleeding.
