AB  -  It's subjunctive history. You know, the subjunctive? The mood used when something may or may not have happened. When it is imagined. (Alan Bennett, The History Boys) The General Medical Council (GMC) guide good medical practice states, 'You must keep your professional knowledge and skills up to date'. Guidelines from the National Institute of Clinical Excellence (NICE) help doctors to provide care according to the best available evidence without sifting through it. However there is a risk that, by focusing only on guidelines and neglecting individual trials, doctors overestimate the strength of evidence on which their practice is based. This in turn prevents them asking important, unanswered questions with an open mind. Considering pulmonary embolism (PE), NICE guidelines (2015) recommend 'offer. . . low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) or fondaparinux'. The advent of direct oral anticoagulant drugs (DOACs) has increased the anticoagulation on offer, but the recommendation to offer anticoagulant therapy to all patients with PE is still based on a single randomised trial (Barritt & Jordan, 1960). This recruited 35 patients before the control arm was closed, and used no imaging beyond chest radiographs. There was no blinding or placebo treatment. Recruitment based on clinical signs for pulmonary infarction likely included patients with infectious pneumonia as well as PE. This trial was preceded by the haphazard introduction, in the 1930s, of anticoagulation with heparin to treat deep vein thrombosis (DVT), according to physicians' preferences. The observed mortality drop in patients with DVT from 18% to 0.4% was generally accepted as compelling evidence. However bedside diagnosis of PE is infamously unreliable, and some physicians withheld heparin due to bleeding risk prior to 1960. The introduction of ventilation‐perfusion (V/Q) scintigraphy in the 1960s made diagnosis of PE more reliable, but it is unreliable for detecting small emboli. CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) was introduced in the early 1990s, and its widespread adoption was associated, in the USA, with an 81% increase in reported PE incidence, but only a very small decrease in mortality. A randomised trial comparing CTPA with V/Q scanning found an increased rate of PE with CTPA, but no corresponding decrease in recurrent venous thromboembolism during the 3 months follow‐up period. (Anderson et al. 2007) These data suggest that the additional, smaller, emboli diagnosed by the more sensitive technology may not be clinically significant, and may not need treatment. Unfortunately, following the advent of CTPA, randomised trials re‐testing anticoagulation against placebo are still awaited. Anticoagulation therapy is associated with significant bleeding risk. In the 1960s trial, one of two deaths in the heparin arm related to a haemorrhagic peptic ulcer, and bleeding affected 27% of heparin patients in the urokinase pulmonary embolism trial (1970). The risk may well outweigh any benefit in patients with subsegmental and asymptomatic PE, but this is unknown. By understanding the history of a treatment and its evolving indications over time, shortcomings in the trial data can become visible. This may not alter clinical practice immediately, but it informs trial design and recruitment to address the critical unknowns in a field, rather than merely seeking to identify a market niche for the latest pharmaceutical variant.
