AB  -  In a trial enrolling 897 patients who had an initial episode of venous thromboembolism, 443 were randomized to receive prophylactic oral anticoagulation for six weeks and 454 for six months. Initial diagnoses as well as recurrent thromboembolic events were confirmed objectively. Anticoagulation therapy for six months resulted in a lower recurrence rate than treatment for six weeks. The difference in the incidence of recurrence between the groups occurred between six weeks and six months after baseline. The difference between the groups remained unchanged for at least the next year and a half.
