DO  -  10.1111/j.1538-7836.2011.04380_1.x
AB  -  Background: Patients with iliofemoral Deep Vein Thrombosis (ifDVT) have a twofold increased chance of developing a Post Thrombotic Syndrome (PTS). Catheter Directed Thrombolysis (CDT) has shown to reduce PTS incidence in case series. Until now there is no unequivocal evidence on long term PTS reduction from randomized controlled studies. Objective: to conduct a randomized controlled trial to assess the value of catheter directed thrombolysis for PTS reduction. Methods: A prospective, randomized controlled multi‐center study comparing ultrasound accelerated CDT with oral anticoagulation therapy (oat) and compressive stockings to oat and compressive stockings alone for acute (< 2weeks) ifDVT. Underlying obstructions will also be treated. One hundred and eighty patients will be included; the study will be performed in eight dutch regional hospitals. The primary outcome measure is PTS incidence after one year. Secondary outcomes are pulmonary embolism, recurrent thrombosis and bleeding. Follow‐up at three, six and twelve months will include clinical signs of PTS scored using the Villalta‐scale and CEAP score. New imaging techniques will be used to reveal underlying anatomical lesions. Occurrence of PTS will be correlated with MR‐venography, venography, duplex and air plethysmography. Also biochemical markers of PTS will be assessed. Conclusion: This study will determine if thrombolysis in acute IFDVT decreases PTS significantly. More insight into underlying anatomical and biochemical parameters that influence PTS will be indentified. At the same time the most appropriate diagnostic imaging techniques will be evaluated.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01003356/full
