AB  -  Background and Aims: Infection of the head or neck is an established cause of pediatric cerebral venous thrombosis (CVT), but data mostly come from retrospective case series. Methods: We performed a subgroup analysis of the EINSTEIN‐Jr trial (2015‐2018), in which 500 children with venous thromboembolism (VTE) of all types were randomized to treatment with rivaroxaban or standard anticoagulants (heparins/vitamin K antagonists). We evaluated children with CVT and an associated head or neck infection. Results: Of 114 children with CVT, 74 (65%) had a head or neck infection and were included. Median age was 6 years (IQR 3‐10); 62% were male. Infections included otomastoiditis (80%), central nervous system infection (28%), sinusitis (24%), or other local infection (12%); 39% had infection of multiple head‐neck regions. The most common symptoms at presentation were headache (72%), fever (57%), and focal neurologic deficits (36%). Thrombosis mostly occurred in the lateral sinus (91%) and the cavernous sinus (10%). Baseline brain imaging demonstrated parenchymal lesion (s) in 8%. All children received antibiotic treatment, and 53% underwent surgical treatment (46% had mastoidectomy). No children died, and all completed the 3‐month follow‐up. Recurrent VTE (1%), major bleeding (1%), and clinically relevant non‐major bleeding (1%) were all rare. At 3 months follow‐up, 4% had persistent focal neurologic deficits, and 3% had decreased vision. Conclusions: Infection of the head or neck was a common risk factor among children with CVT. Outcome of children with head or neck infection‐associated CVT was generally favorable, with low risks of VTE recurrence, bleeding complications, persistent focal neurologic deficits, or decreased vision.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-02338858/full
