AB  -  Introduction: Historically reserved for the treatment of reflux of the great saphenous vein due to neurological risks, radiofrequency ablation is also proposed in the treatment of reflux of the Short Saphenous Vein. Compare With the LASER, the literature is very poor about short and long‐term results concerning the treatment of the short saphenous vein by radiofrequency ablation. The aim of this study is to compare short and long‐terms outcomes of a group of patients operated by crossectomie‐stripping versus a group treated by radiofrequency ablation in terms of paresthesia and postoperative deep vein thrombosis, stop working, venous occlusion and recurrence. Methods: Between January 2012 and December 2013, 146 patients were included consecutively without randomization: 62 patents (67 Legs) in the surgery group, 84 patients (97 legs) in the radiofrequency ablation group. Phlebectomies was associated in 100 % of cases. The choice of the technique was dictated by the diameter of the vein and the distance between the skin and the vein. A clinical and echographic evaluation was made at 1 week, 6, 12, 24 and 48 months. The end points was occlusion of treated vein and recurrence. Results: The mean follow‐up in both groups was 46 months. There was no failure or recanalisation. Five clinical recurrences were observed: 2 in the Radiofrequency ablation group,. 3 In the surgery group (p= 0.4). One patient in the Radiofrequency ablation group, 5 in the surgery group had paresthesiae (p= 0.07).No DVT were identified. Two patients of the surgical group had a local thrombosis of gastrocnemius veins, without sequellae after anticoagulation. Stop Working was statically lower in the radiofrequency ablation group (3.9 days versus 6.7 days: p < 0.01). Conclusion: The treatment of reflux of Short saphenous Vein by Radiofrequency ablation is safe, less aggressive and at least as effective as the surgical treatment. It can therefore be proposed In first intention. Disclosure: Nothing to disclose
