AB  -  Introduction: Thrombolysis has long been considered a potentially life‐saving intervention for patients with acute pulmonary embolism (PE) accompanied by right‐ventricular dysfunction and shock (massive PE). However, there is evidence that this therapy may be underutilized because of concerns for major bleeding. Case report: Our patient was a 46 year old man with a history of mild cognitive impairment who had been admitted to an outside hospital for treatment of osteomyelitis involving his left heel. He subsequently developed hemodynamically significant bleeding from a duodenal ulcer that did not respond to endoscopic management and for which he eventually underwent antrectomy and proximal duodenectomy. When it became clear that the surgery had failed to control his bleeding, he was transferred to our Intensive Care Unit. Eventually, the bleeding subsided with conservative management, but his course was complicated by delirium and multiple transfers between the floor and the ICU because of recurrent aspiration. Three weeks after his last episode of bleeding, he developed acute hypoxemia and was re‐intubated. Bedside bronchoscopy was unremarkable, but an arterial blood gas on Fi02 0.1 revealed an A‐a gradient of 558. A PE protocol CT showed a massive, centrally‐located PE with signs of marked right heart strain, and a bedside echocardiogram showed right ventricular hypokinesis with preserved apical function (McConnell's Sign). Because of his high risk of major bleeding, we treated him initially with heparin only. However, his hypoxemia persisted and he became hypotensive, at which time we administered tPA. Within two hours, his hypotension resolved and his Fi02 was weaned. He survived until discharge without any signs of major bleeding. Discussion: Since a 1995 randomized controlled trial of streptokinase plus heparin versus heparin alone for patients with acute PE and shock was terminated early because of unacceptable mortality in the heparin group, thrombolysis has been considered a life‐saving treatment for patients with massive PE. Nevertheless, the largest observational study of acute PE management demonstrated that thrombolytics were administered in only one‐third of patients with massive PE. This is almost certainly attributed to associated rates of major bleeding that may approach 20%. Nevertheless, our case provides one example of a patient who benefitted from thrombolysis despite a significantly elevated risk of GI bleeding, and should prompt clinicians to consider thrombolysis more strongly in all patients with massive PE.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01091733/full
