DO  -  10.1093/eurheartj/ehx502.P5136
AB  -  Introduction: Betrixaban (Btx) is a direct inhibitor of factor Xa (FXa) investigated for prevention of venous thromboembolism (VTE) in patients who are at risk due to acute medical illness. A completed Phase 3 trial (APEX) compared extended duration anticoagulation with Btx to the standard‐of‐care enoxaparin in acute medically ill patients. Purpose: To analyze the potential effect of patient characteristics on population pharmacokinetics (PK) and exposure‐response relationships for acute medically ill patients who received Btx in the APEX study Methods: In the APEX study, patients received Btx (35‐42 days; n=3759) or enoxaparin (10±4 days; n=3754). The primary efficacy endpoint was the composite occurrence of VTE events (asymptomatic proximal DVT, symptomatic DVT, non‐fatal PE, or VTE‐related death). The primary safety outcome was the occurrence of major bleeding as defined by ISTH criteria; secondary endpoints included the occurrence of clinically relevant non‐major (CRNM) bleeding. Btx dose was 80 mg PO QD; for patients with severe renal insufficiency (≥15 to <30 CrCl mL/min) or requiring a concomitant strong P‐glycoprotein (P‐gp) inhibitor, the dose was reduced to 40 mg PO QD. One PK sample per patient was collected at the day of hospital discharge (no later than Day 14 after randomization); samples were taken 0‐5 h or 10‐30 h after the most recent dose of study medication. Patient characteristics included age, sex, race, region (Europe/North America/Latin America/Other) body weight, CrCl category (≥15 to <30, ≥30 to <60, ≥60 to <90, ≥120 mL/min), and specific P‐gp inhibitor. Results: In total, 3146 PK samples from patients who received Btx were analyzed. For the 80‐mg dose, the projected concentration was 18.8 ng/mL at 2‐h post‐dose and 16.1 ng/mL at 20‐h post‐dose, showing a stable daily concentra‐tion level on QD dosing. Co‐administration of two P‐gp inhibitors (amiodarone or clarithromycin) on the day of sampling more than doubled Btx concentration to ∼35 ng/mL at 20‐h post‐dosing. For the 40‐mg dose, the projected concentration was 7.2 ng/mL at 20‐h post‐dose, indicating a greater‐than‐dose‐proportional exposure relationship. Patient age, sex, weight, CrCl category, P‐gp inhibitors, and region were identified as significant covariates affecting Btx PK. The exposureresponse relationship for the primary efficacy endpoint was not significant, but the relationship between Btx concentration and major or CRNM bleeding was significant in multivariate testing (p=0.011). Conclusions: The Btx PK profile exhibited stable serum concentration levels with QD dosing. Several covariates had a small effect (15‐20%) on Btx concentration, but had no effect on efficacy or safety. Due to increased observed concentrations, Btx dose should be adjusted to 40 mg for patients taking amiodarone or clarithromycin, but not other P‐gp inhibitors. Adjustments of Btx dose should not be necessary for patients characterized by other covariates tested in this analysis.
UR  -  https://www.cochranelibrary.com/central/doi/10.1002/central/CN-01468675/full
