AB  -  Introduction: Bronchiectasis is a condition associated with pathological dilatations of bronchi with inflammation and chronic infection. This condition predisposes patients to recurrent hemoptysis. As anticoagulation is associated with an increased risk of bleeding, the medical conditions associated with a high risk for pulmonary bleeding are not well described. The MAGELLAN study evaluated in‐hospital and extended thromboprophylaxis with rivaroxaban 10mg once‐daily (QD) for 35 ±4 days compared with enoxaparin 40mg QD for 10 ±4 days followed by placebo (enoxaparin/placebo) in medically ill patients including patients with bronchiectasis at baseline. The MAGELLAN study demonstrated the efficacy of rivaroxaban in reducing the risk of venous thromboembolism. However, the benefit‐risk profile did not favor rivaroxaban due to an increase in major and fatal bleeding. The aim of this analysis was to assess the risk of pulmonary bleeding in subjects with bronchiectasis in the MAGELLAN study. Methods: The subgroup analyzed were hospitalized subjects enrolled in the MAGELLAN study with bronchiectasis at baseline. Patients were evaluated by treatment group for ISTH major bleeding, non‐major clinical relevant (NMCR) bleeding and the composite of the two (ie clinically relevant bleeding). Results: A total of 121 subjects with bronchiectasis were randomized to either rivaroxaban (n=60) or enoxaparin/placebo (n=61). There were 3 major bleeds, all fatal, in the rivaroxaban group (2 pulmonary, 1 gastrointestinal) and none in the enoxaparin/placebo group, with all occurring within the first 10 days of therapy. There was 1 NMCR bleed in the rivaroxaban group and 2 in the enoxaparin/placebo group. Thus, the incidence of clinically relevant bleeding was 6.67% vs 3.3% in the rivaroxaban and enoxaparin/placebo/placebo groups, respectively. Conclusion: Clinical relevant bleeding, including fatal pulmonary hemorrhage, tended to occur more frequently with extended rivaroxaban thromboprophylaxis than with enoxaparin/ followed by placebo in patients with bronchiectasis. The risks of anticoagulation must be considered carefully in hospitalized medically ill patients with bronchiectasis.
