AB  -  Background/Synopsis: Statin therapy and lower LDL‐c levels have been observed to potentially increase risk of intracerebral hemorrhage (ICH). However observational studies and randomized clinical trials have yielded conflicting results. Patients with ASCVD risk factors who suffer an ICH pose a clinical challenge as statin association with ICH is unclear. Objective/Purpose: We highlight the knowledge gap related to safe use of statin therapy through a case exemplifying the clinical challenge of managing ASCVD risk factors post‐ICH. Methods: 75‐year‐old female with history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, and subclinical ASCVD with a coronary artery calcium score of 450, presented to the hospital with severe headache and was diagnosed with acute right ICH. She did not smoke, use illicit drugs, drink excess alcohol, or have family history of premature ASCVD. Outpatient medications included amlodipine, rosuvastatin, and aspirin. She incidentally stopped aspirin 3 months prior to this stroke. Her lipid panel at time of hospitalization was total cholesterol 130 mg/dL, HDL 38 mg/dL, TG 137 mg/dL and LDL‐C 70 mg/dL. Imaging studies were negative for cortical venous thrombosis, reversible cerebrovascular constriction syndrome, vasculitis, aneurysm, AV fistula, or arterio‐venous malformations as etiologies for her hemorrhage. TEE was negative for cardioembolic source. Her hypertension medications were optimized, and she was discharged without any statin therapy. She recovered with mild residual neurological deficits. Results: She was enrolled in an open‐label randomized study, the SATURN trial which aims to assess safety and association of statin use and recurrent ICH. Her lipid panel while off statins showed a total cholesterol of 274 mg/dL, triglycerides 159 mg/dL, LDL of 197 mg/dL, and HDL 47 mg/dL. She presented to the lipid clinic for advice on cardiovascular risk reduction and consideration for use of PCSK9 inhibitors. Conclusions: Historically, early epidemiological studies in the 1980s based on large registries suggested association between statin use and very low LDL‐C levels with recurrent ICH. Concerns were sparked after post‐hoc analyses of SPARCL and the Heart Protection study suggested similar association of statin use post‐ICH and post‐TIA with recurrent ICH. Other epidemiological studies with large registries, mendelian randomization studies and meta‐analysis of randomized and observational trials have not shown a clear association. Certain genetic variants, most notably related to Apo E, have been shown to contribute to ICH risk. Managing patients ASCVD risks post‐ICH pose a clinical challenge. There is conflicting data between observational studies and randomized trials about the role and safety of statin use post‐ICH. There are no societal guideline recommendations to guide clinicians on statin use and goal LDL‐c levels post‐ICH in patients with ASCVD. There is some evidence to suggest PCSK9 inhibitors may be safer in terms of less propensity to increase risk of ICH however this is largely unknown, and guidelines do not comment on this specifically. Hopefully future studies including the current open label randomized SATURN trial will help guide clinicians regarding statin use post‐ICH. External Funding: No
