AB  -  Background: Antiplatelet therapy is used to prevent thrombosis in the arterial and anticoagulant therapy in the venous system. Arteriovenous fistula (AVF) has characteristics of both vascular systems. We investigated the effect of combined antithrombotic therapy (low dose, lowmolecular‐ weight heparin, and antiplatelet therapy) compared to antiplatelet therapy alone after percutaneous transluminal angioplasty (PTA) of AVF stenosis. Materials and methods: We conducted a prospective, randomized pilot trial. Patients with AVF stenosis were treated with PTA and randomized to combined antithrombotic or antiplatelet therapy. The primary endpoint was lumen loss in the index trial area on ultrasound at 6 weeks, 3, 6, and 12 months. Secondary endpoints were AVF volume flow reduction and reintervention to the index trial area within 12 months. The primary safety endpoint was freedom from adverse events (bleeding, ischemia, infection, death). Results: We enrolled patients between October 2019 and September 2021. The epidemic resulted in a lower number of included patients. 79 stenoses were randomized to antiplatelet therapy and 81 to combined antithrombotic therapy. PTA was done with plain or drug‐coated balloons (DCB), stents, or stent‐grafts. No difference in restenosis rate was observed (antiplatelet arm 27.8%, combined antithrombotic therapy arm 24.7%, p=0.650). A decline in AVF volume flow was observed in almost all groups. 68 (42.5%) patients had reintervention to the index trial area. There was no significant difference in patency, however, we observed a non‐significant tendency to longer primary patency in the combined antithrombotic therapy group for DCB and stent subgroups. We observed no significant difference in minor bleeding complications and no major bleeding. Conclusion: Combined antithrombotic therapy showed no increased bleeding risk compared to antiplatelet therapy alone. There was no significant difference in restenosis rate in the index trial area.
